  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1233 PM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nThe region sits in a relatively dry and benign pattern this \nafternoon, with light northeast winds pushing out of high \npressure centered over Ohio. High temperatures are working into \nthe mid 70s and relatively stable weather looks likely for the \nnext several days. Max temps will creep upwards through the next\ncouple of days, likely pushing above 80 each day but the \nmoderate northeast flow and lack of any return flow should keep \nhumidity levels comfortable.\n\nThe tropics may produce the next notable weather element for the\nregion. A system currently in the southern Bay of Campeche has\nthe potential to become more organized over the next few days.\nA compilation of global models shows something moving into the\nnorth central Gulf by Saturday and possibly impacting this area\nSunday or Monday. The actual final intensity and organization of\nany system that forms would have substantial influence on any\ntrack or impacts the system brings, particularly for this area.\n30-40% rain chances enter the forecast on Sunday and if current\nmodel trends hold those would be on track to increase as we get\ncloser.",
